<chapter
 id="lecture10-10">
#title("Example: Queen Puzzle Revisited")

#para("\

The famous 8-queen puzzle asks the player to find ways to put eight
queen pieces on a chess board such that no queen piece can attack any
other ones. In other words, no two queen pieces can be put on the same
row, the same column, or the same diagnal. A solution to a generalized
version of this puzzle was presented in an earlier part of the book
but there is a serious drawback with the solution when it is used to
animate the underlying search process. For instance, if we want to
deal with the case where the chess board is of the dimension 20 by 20,
then no animation can actually happen as the time taken to generate
all of the valid board configurations to the puzzle is prohibitively
long. By making use of stream-based lazy-evaluation, I present as
follows a slight variant of the previously presented solution so as
to obviate the need for generating all of the valid board
configurations before displaying them.  Instead, each valid board
configuration is generated only when it needs to be displayed.\

")

#para("\

Please recall that a node represents a board configuration where there
are #emphasis("n") queen pieces on the first #emphasis("n") rows of
the chess board such that no one piece can attack any other
pieces. Given a node, another node extending it with one more queen
piece is considered its child. A node is a linear list of integers, and
the functions #dyncode("node_get_children"), #dyncode("node_free") and so on are
those of the earlier chapter (the file #filename("queens.hats") of the code of that chapter
is included). The function #dyncode("node_get_children") is called to obtain
all of the child nodes of a given node, which is borrowed.

")

#para("\

With
#dyncode("node_get_children"),
we can readily implement #dyncode("node_dfsenum") as follows
for enumerating in the depth-first manner all of the nodes contained
in the tree rooted at a given node. The return value of
#dyncode("node_dfsenum") is a (linear) stream, in contrast to the list used in the
previous version, and the stream owns the node. The thunk of the stream is where
the children of the node are computed, which happens only when the stream is
evaluated, and the second piece of code of #keycode("$ldelay") frees the node if
the stream is freed without having been evaluated. Three functions are declared first:

#dats2xhtml('\
//
extern
fun
node_dfsenum
  (nx0: node): stream_vt(node)
//
// a list of streams is flattened lazily into a stream
//
extern
fun
list_stream_concat
  (xss: List0_vt(stream_vt(node))): stream_vt(node)
//
// each node of a list of nodes is turned into the stream of
// the nodes of the tree rooted at the node (nothing is
// enumerated at this point)
//
extern
fun
nodes_dfsenum
  (nxs: List0_vt(node)): List0_vt(stream_vt(node))
//
')

#dats2xhtml('\
//
implement
node_dfsenum(nx0) = $ldelay
(
let
  val nxs = node_get_children(nx0)
  val xss = nodes_dfsenum(nxs)
in
  stream_vt_cons(nx0, list_stream_concat(xss))
end // end of [let]
,
node_free(nx0) // called when the stream is freed
) (* node_dfsenum *)
//
implement
nodes_dfsenum(nxs) =
(
case+ nxs of
| ~list_vt_nil() => list_vt_nil()
| ~list_vt_cons(nx, nxs) =>
  list_vt_cons(node_dfsenum(nx), nodes_dfsenum(nxs))
)
//
')

Note that the function #dyncode("nodes_dfsenum") turns each node of a list of
nodes into a stream of nodes, and it does so lazily: the function
#dyncode("node_dfsenum") returns at once with a thunk. The list of the
streams is then flattened into a single stream by the function
#dyncode("list_stream_concat"), which is a variant of the function
#dyncode("list_concat") of the chapter on list-processing:

#dats2xhtml('\
//
fun
streams_free
(xss: List0_vt(stream_vt(node))): void =
(
case+ xss of
| ~list_vt_nil() => ()
| ~list_vt_cons(xs, xss) => (~xs; streams_free(xss))
)
//
//
// the version of [stream_append] for the streams of nodes
// (a node is linear)
//
fun
stream_append_nodes
(xs: stream_vt(node), ys: stream_vt(node)): stream_vt(node) = $ldelay
(
(
case+ !xs of
| ~stream_vt_nil() => !ys
| ~stream_vt_cons(x, xs) =>
  stream_vt_cons(x, stream_append_nodes(xs, ys))
) : stream_vt_con(node)
,
(~xs; ~ys)
)
//
implement
list_stream_concat(xss) = $ldelay
(
(
case+ xss of
| ~list_vt_nil() => stream_vt_nil()
| ~list_vt_cons(xs, xss) => !(stream_append_nodes(xs, list_stream_concat(xss)))
) : stream_vt_con(node)
,
streams_free(xss)
) (* list_stream_concat *)
//
')

Given a list of streams, #dyncode("list_stream_concat") flattens it
lazily into a single stream. The function
#dyncode("stream_append_nodes") is the function #dyncode("stream_append") of the
chapter on streams (a node is linear and so the elements of the streams of this
chapter cannot be those of the functions of that chapter, which are about
non-linear elements); the streams of the list that is not consumed are
freed by #dyncode("streams_free") if the flattened stream is freed.

")

#para("\

In order to find the solutions to the 8-queen puzzle, we just need to keep the nodes of
length 8 that are contained in the stream. The nodes that are not
kept are freed:

#dats2xhtml('\
//
// Only the nodes of length N are kept: the others are freed
//
fun
keep_solutions
(nxs: stream_vt(node)): stream_vt(node) = $ldelay
(
(
case+ !nxs of
| ~stream_vt_nil() => stream_vt_nil()
| ~stream_vt_cons(nx, nxs) =>
  if node_length(nx) = N
    then stream_vt_cons(nx, keep_solutions(nxs))
    else (node_free(nx); !(keep_solutions(nxs)))
) : stream_vt_con(node)
,
~nxs
)
//
fun
QueenSolve(): stream_vt(node) =
keep_solutions(node_dfsenum(node_init()))
//
')

The first solution is obtained by evaluating the stream once, and the
rest of the stream is not enumerated until it is requested:

#dats2xhtml('\
implement
main0() = {
//
val sols = QueenSolve()
//
// the first solution is found without enumerating the others
//
val-~stream_vt_cons(nx, sols) = !sols
val () = println! ("the first solution: ", nx)
val () = node_free(nx)
//
// the other solutions are counted (and freed) one by one
//
fun
count(sols: stream_vt(node), res: int): int =
(
case+ !sols of
| ~stream_vt_nil() => res
| ~stream_vt_cons(nx, sols) => (node_free(nx); count(sols, res+1))
)
//
val () = println! ("the other solutions: ", count(sols, 0))
//
} (* end of [main0] *)
')

The first line printed is the first solution found (the columns of the queens, the last
queen first), after a small part of the tree has been enumerated, and the
second line shows that the other 91 solutions are still there. A demo for
animating the depth-first search performed by #dyncode("node_dfsenum") was
available <ulink
url=\"https://ats-lang.github.io/DOCUMENT/ATS2FUNCRASH/LECTURE/10-10/CODE/QueenPuzzle.html\">here</ulink>
(the GC-era version).

")

#para("\

Please find #mycodelink("LECTURE/10-10/CODE", "on-line") the entirety
of the code used in this chapter. The file #filename("QueenPuzzle.dats") was compiled with
#command("patscc") (no flag is needed) and run with the address sanitizer,
which reports no leak (the stream that is freed before it is evaluated frees
the nodes it holds).

")#comment("para")

</chapter><!--id="lecture10-10"-->

